1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,837
Chapter 21: Physiology of Labor.

3
00:00:05,837 --> 00:00:22,675
Maternal And Fetal Compartments Sex Steroid Hormone Role Prostaglandins Role PHASE 1: UTERINE QUIESCENCE AND CERVICAL SOFTENING PHASE 2: PREPARATION FOR LABOR PHASE 3: LABOR UTEROTONINS IN PARTURITION PHASE 3 PHASE 4: THE PUERPERIUM References.

4
00:00:22,675 --> 00:00:31,837
Labor is characterized by forceful and painful uterine contractions that effect cervical dilation and cause the fetus to descend through the birth canal.

5
00:00:31,837 --> 00:00:38,075
However, extensive preparations take place in both the uterus and cervix long before this.

6
00:00:38,075 --> 00:00:46,087
During the first 36 to 38 weeks of normal gestation, the myometrium is in a preparatory yet unresponsive state.

7
00:00:46,087 --> 00:00:52,550
Concurrently, the cervix begins an early stage of remodeling yet maintains structural integrity.

8
00:00:52,550 --> 00:01:04,662
Following uterine quiescence, a transitional phase begins during which myometrial unresponsiveness is suspended and the cervix undergoes ripening, effacement, and loss of structural cohesion.

9
00:01:04,662 --> 00:01:11,362
The physiological processes that regulate parturition and the onset of labor continue to be defined.

10
00:01:11,362 --> 00:01:14,637
Three theories describe labor initiation.

11
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:20,275
Viewed simplistically, the first is the functional loss of pregnancy maintenance factors.

12
00:01:20,275 --> 00:01:25,037
The second focuses on synthesis of factors that induce parturition.

13
00:01:25,037 --> 00:01:31,362
The third suggests that the mature fetus is the source of the initial signal for parturition commencement.

14
00:01:31,362 --> 00:01:35,387
Research supports a model that draws from all three themes.

15
00:01:35,387 --> 00:01:42,637
Thus, labor onset represents the culmination of a series of biochemical changes in the uterus and cervix.

16
00:01:42,637 --> 00:01:48,450
These result from endocrine and paracrine signals emanating from both mother and fetus.

17
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:58,362
Their relative contributions vary between species, and it is these differences that complicate elucidation of the exact factors that regulate human parturition.

18
00:01:58,362 --> 00:02:05,125
When parturition is abnormal, preterm labor, dystocia, or postterm pregnancy may result.

19
00:02:05,125 --> 00:02:07,837
Maternal And Fetal Compartments.

20
00:02:07,837 --> 00:02:10,575
# Uterus and Cervix.

21
00:02:10,575 --> 00:02:17,562
The myometrial layer of the uterus is composed of bundles of smooth muscle cells surrounded by connective tissue.

22
00:02:17,562 --> 00:02:27,750
In contrast to skeletal or cardiac muscle, the smooth muscle cell is not terminally differentiated and therefore is readily adaptable to environmental changes.

23
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:42,562
Varied stimuli such as mechanical stretch, inflammation, and endocrine and paracrine signals modulate the transition of the smooth muscle cell into phenotypes that provide cell growth, proliferation, secretion, and contractility.

24
00:02:42,562 --> 00:02:50,325
Additionally, several smooth muscle qualities confer advantages for uterine contraction efficiency and fetal delivery.

25
00:02:50,325 --> 00:02:59,225
First, the degree of smooth muscle cell shortening with contractions may be one order of magnitude greater than that attained in striated muscle cells.

26
00:02:59,225 --> 00:03:04,625
Second, forces can be exerted in smooth muscle cells in multiple directions.

27
00:03:04,625 --> 00:03:12,500
This differs from the contraction force generated by skeletal muscle, which is always aligned with the axis of the muscle fibers.

28
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:19,212
Third, the thick and thin filaments of smooth muscle are found in long, random bundles throughout the cells.

29
00:03:19,212 --> 00:03:24,787
This plexiform arrangement aids augmented shortening and force-generating capacity.

30
00:03:24,787 --> 00:03:34,625
Last, greater multidirectional force generation in the uterine fundus compared with that of the lower uterine segment helps optimize expulsive force vectors.

31
00:03:34,625 --> 00:03:42,812
Lining the thick muscular uterine walls, the endometrium is transformed by pregnancy hormones and is then termed decidua.

32
00:03:42,812 --> 00:03:54,362
Composed of stromal cells and maternal immune cells, the decidua serves to maintain the pregnancy via unique immunoregulatory functions that suppress inflammatory signals during gestation.

33
00:03:54,362 --> 00:04:05,412
However, at the end of pregnancy, the decidua transitions to induce inflammatory signals and withdraw active immunosuppression, contributing to parturition initiation.

34
00:04:05,412 --> 00:04:28,575
During pregnancy, the cervix has multiple functions that include: (1) maintaining the epithelial barrier to protect the reproductive tract from infection, (2) sustaining cervical competence despite greater gravitational forces as the fetus grows, and (3) orchestrating extracellular matrix (ECM) changes that allow progressively greater tissue compliance.

35
00:04:28,575 --> 00:04:30,500
In nonpregnant

36
00:04:30,652 --> 00:04:36,802
women, the cervix is closed and firm, and its consistency is similar to nasal cartilage.

37
00:04:36,752 --> 00:04:44,514
By the end of pregnancy, the cervix is easily distensible, and its consistency is similar to the lips of the oral cavity.

38
00:04:44,514 --> 00:04:53,302
The cervix has a high ratio of fibroblasts to smooth muscle cells, and ECM contributes significantly to overall tissue mass.

39
00:04:53,302 --> 00:05:00,289
Vink and colleagues (2016) showed that the smooth muscle cells in the cervix have a spatial gradient.

40
00:05:00,289 --> 00:05:09,764
Specifically, smooth muscle cells make up approximately 50 percent of stromal cells at the internal os but only 10 percent at the external os.

41
00:05:09,764 --> 00:05:27,327
Also, three-dimensional sonography and magnetic resonance imaging show increases in the cross-sectional area of the cervical canal and changes in ECM structure from early to late pregnancy (House, 2009; Lang, 2010; Pizzella, 2020).

42
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:35,389
Concurrent with expansion of the stroma, the cervical epithelia proliferate and exert a pregnancy-specific immunoprotection.

43
00:05:35,389 --> 00:05:37,514
# Placenta.

44
00:05:37,514 --> 00:05:52,414
In addition to providing the exchange of nutrients and waste between mother and fetus, the placenta is a key source of steroid hormones, growth factors, and other mediators that maintain pregnancy and potentially aid the transition to parturition.

45
00:05:52,414 --> 00:06:04,164
The fetal membranes—amnion and chorion—and adjacent decidua serve as a physiologic, immunologic, and metabolic shield to protect against untimely parturition initiation.

46
00:06:04,164 --> 00:06:11,139
The amnion provides virtually all of the fetal membranes’ tensile strength to resist membrane tearing and rupture .

47
00:06:11,139 --> 00:06:19,064
This avascular tissue is highly resistant to penetration by leukocytes, microorganisms, and neoplastic cells .

48
00:06:19,064 --> 00:06:27,339
It also constitutes a selective filter to prevent fetal particulate-bound lung and skin secretions from reaching the maternal compartment.

49
00:06:27,339 --> 00:06:40,652
In this manner, maternal tissues are protected from amnionic fluid constituents that could prematurely accelerate decidual or myometrial activation or could promote adverse events such as amnionic fluid embolism.

50
00:06:40,652 --> 00:06:43,252
Figure 21-1.

51
00:06:43,252 --> 00:06:56,439
The amnion synthesizes prostaglandins, and late in pregnancy, synthesis is augmented by increased phospholipase A2 and prostaglandin H synthase, type 2 (PGHS-2) activity.

52
00:06:56,439 --> 00:07:08,164
The transport of prostaglandins from the amnion to maternal tissues is limited by expression of the inactivating enzyme, prostaglandin dehydrogenase (PGDH), in the chorion.

53
00:07:08,164 --> 00:07:17,564
During labor, PGDH levels decline, and amnion-derived prostaglandins can influence membrane rupture and uterine contractility.

54
00:07:17,564 --> 00:07:31,777
The role of decidual activation in parturition is unclear but may involve local progesterone metabolism and higher prostaglandin receptor concentrations, thus enhancing uterine prostaglandin actions and cytokine production.

55
00:07:31,777 --> 00:07:35,052
Redrawn from Smith R: Parturition.

56
00:07:35,052 --> 00:07:43,102
N Engl J Med. 2007 Jan 18;356(3):271–283.

57
00:07:43,102 --> 00:07:49,164
The chorion is a primarily protective tissue layer and provides immunological acceptance.

58
00:07:49,164 --> 00:07:56,327
It is also enriched with enzymes that inactivate uterotonins, which are agents that stimulate contractions.

59
00:07:56,327 --> 00:08:08,952
Inactivating enzymes include prostaglandin dehydrogenase, oxytocinase, and enkephalinase (Cheung, 1990; Germain, 1994; Mizutani, 2011).

60
00:08:08,952 --> 00:08:11,552
Sex Steroid Hormone Role.

61
00:08:11,552 --> 00:08:20,539
In many species, the role of sex steroid hormones is clear— estrogen promotes and progesterone inhibits the events leading to parturition.

62
00:08:20,539 --> 00:08:27,827
The removal of progesterone, that is, progesterone withdrawal, directly precedes progression of parturition.

63
00:08:27,827 --> 00:08:37,527
In addition, providing progesterone to some species will delay parturition via a decline in myometrial activity and continued cervical competency .

64
00:08:37,527 --> 00:08:45,977
In humans, however, both estrogen and progesterone are components of a broader molecular system that maintains uterine quiescence.

65
00:08:45,977 --> 00:08:54,577
Plasma levels of estrogen and progesterone in normal pregnancy are enormous and in great excess of the affinity constants for their receptors.

66
00:08:54,577 --> 00:09:04,902
For this reason, it is difficult to comprehend how relatively subtle changes in the ratio of their concentrations could modulate physiological processes during pregnancy.

67
00:09:04,902 --> 00:09:14,452
The evidence, however, for an increased progesterone-toestrogen ratio in the maintenance of pregnancy and a decline in this ratio for parturition is

68
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:16,370
overwhelming.

69
00:09:16,320 --> 00:09:29,895
In all species studied, including humans, administration of the progesterone-receptor antagonists mifepristone (RU486) or onapristone will promote some or all key features of parturition.

70
00:09:29,895 --> 00:09:43,832
These include cervical ripening, greater cervical distensibility, and augmented uterine sensitivity to uterotonins (Bygdeman, 1994; Chwalisz, 1994b; Wolf, 1993).

71
00:09:43,832 --> 00:09:51,532
The exact role of estrogen in regulation of human uterine quiescence and cervical competency is less well understood.

72
00:09:51,532 --> 00:10:01,932
That said, estrogen can advance progesterone responsiveness and, at the end of pregnancy, aid in processes that mediate uterine activation and cervical ripening.

73
00:10:01,932 --> 00:10:08,070
Both progesterone and estrogen bind to nuclear receptors that regulate gene transcription.

74
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:15,807
Two nuclear receptors for estrogen are estrogen receptor α (ERα) and estrogen receptor β (ERβ).

75
00:10:15,807 --> 00:10:24,970
Nuclear receptor isoforms of the progesterone receptor (PR-A and PR-B) are encoded by differing transcripts from a single gene .

76
00:10:24,970 --> 00:10:27,182
Prostaglandins Role.

77
00:10:27,182 --> 00:10:31,970
Prostaglandins are lipid molecules with varied hormone-like actions.

78
00:10:31,970 --> 00:10:39,407
In parturition, they play a prominent role in myometrial contractility, relaxation, and inflammation.

79
00:10:39,407 --> 00:10:53,670
Prostaglandins interact with a family of eight different G protein–coupled receptors (p. 403), several of which are expressed in the myometrium and the cervix (Konopka, 2015; Myatt, 2004).

80
00:10:53,670 --> 00:11:00,657
The major synthetic pathway involved in prostaglandin biosynthesis is shown in Figure 21-2.

81
00:11:00,657 --> 00:11:10,457
Prostaglandins are produced using plasma membrane– derived arachidonic acid, which usually is released by the action of phospholipase A2 or C.

82
00:11:10,457 --> 00:11:26,357
The enzymes type 1 and 2 prostaglandin H synthase (PGHS-1 and -2), also known as cyclooxygenase 1 and 2 (COX-1 and -2), convert arachidonic acid to prostaglandin H2 (PGH2).

83
00:11:26,357 --> 00:11:32,445
These enzymes are the target of many nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).

84
00:11:32,445 --> 00:11:47,395
The tocolytic actions of specific COX inhibitors, as discussed in Chapter 45 (p. 806), were considered promising until they were shown to have adverse fetal effects (Loudon, 2003; Olson, 2007).

85
00:11:47,395 --> 00:11:49,970
Figure 21-2.

86
00:11:49,970 --> 00:11:53,982
Overview of the prostaglandin biosynthetic pathway.

87
00:11:53,982 --> 00:12:21,870
PG = prostaglandin; PGDH = prostaglandin dehydrogenase; PGE2 = prostaglandin E2; PGF2α = prostaglandin F2α; PGH2 = prostaglandin H2; PGHS = prostaglandin H synthase; PGI2 = prostaglandin I2; PLA2 = phospholipase A2; PLC = phospholipase C.

88
00:12:21,870 --> 00:12:28,245
Through prostaglandin isomerases, PGH2 is converted to active prostaglandins.

89
00:12:28,245 --> 00:12:37,382
These include prostaglandins E2 (PGE2), F2α (PGF2α), and I2 (PGI2).

90
00:12:37,382 --> 00:12:44,607
Isomerase expression is tissue-specific and thereby controls the relative production of various prostaglandins.

91
00:12:44,607 --> 00:12:55,757
Another important control point for prostaglandin activity is its metabolism, which most often is through the action of 15-hydroxyprostaglandin dehydrogenase (PGDH).

92
00:12:55,757 --> 00:13:09,882
Expression of this enzyme is upregulated during pregnancy in the uterus and cervix, which provides the important ability to rapidly inactivate prostaglandins (Giannoulias, 2002; Kishore, 2017).

93
00:13:09,882 --> 00:13:32,257
Thus, myometrial responses to prostaglandins stem from a balance between prostaglandin synthesis versus metabolism, from the relative expression of various prostaglandin receptors, or from a switch in receptor-signaling pathways (Kandola, 2014; Lyall, 2002; Olson, 2007; Smith, 2001).

94
00:13:32,257 --> 00:13:42,495
It is possible that prostanoids contribute to myometrial relaxation at one stage of pregnancy and to myometrial contractions after parturition initiation .

95
00:13:42,495 --> 00:13:54,670
In addition to the myometrium, the amnion synthesizes several bioactive peptides and prostaglandins that cause myometrial relaxation or contraction (see Fig. 21-1).

96
00:13:54,670 --> 00:14:07,120
Late in pregnancy, amnionic prostaglandin biosynthesis of PGE2 and PGF2α is increased, and phospholipase A2 and PGHS-2 show greater activity .

97
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:16,945
The amnion is likely the major source for amnionic fluid prostaglandins, and their role in the activation of cascades that promote membrane rupture is clear.

98
00:14:16,945 --> 00:14:28,932
However, the influence of amnionderived prostaglandins on uterine quiescence and activation is less clear because their access to maternal tissues is limited by expression of PGDH.

99
00:14:28,932 --> 00:14:32,532
PHASE 1: UTERINE QUIESCENCE AND CERVICAL

100
00:14:32,692 --> 00:14:34,342
Softening.

101
00:14:34,292 --> 00:14:51,092
Parturition can be arbitrarily divided into four overlapping phases that correspond to the major physiological transitions of the myometrium and cervix during pregnancy (Casey, 1997; Shynlova, 2020; Vink, 2018).

102
00:14:51,092 --> 00:14:59,354
These include: (1) the prelude, (2) the preparation, (3) the process itself, and (4) the recovery.

103
00:14:59,354 --> 00:15:05,604
Importantly, the phases of parturition should not be confused with the clinical stages of labor.

104
00:15:05,604 --> 00:15:11,342
The first, second, and third stages of labor make up phase 3 of parturition .

105
00:15:11,342 --> 00:15:13,967
Figure 21-3.

106
00:15:13,967 --> 00:15:16,454
The phases of parturition.

107
00:15:16,454 --> 00:15:19,104
Figure 21-4.

108
00:15:19,104 --> 00:15:26,842
Labor course divided on the basis of expected evolution of the dilatation and descent curves into three functional divisions.

109
00:15:26,842 --> 00:15:31,267
The preparatory division includes the latent and active phases.

110
00:15:31,267 --> 00:15:36,104
The dilatational division is the phase of maximum slope of dilatation.

111
00:15:36,104 --> 00:15:45,267
The pelvic division encompasses both the deceleration phase and the second stage, which is concurrent with the phase of maximum slope of fetal descent.

112
00:15:45,267 --> 00:15:51,442
Redrawn from Friedman EA: Labor: Clinical Evaluation and Management, 2nd ed.

113
00:15:51,442 --> 00:15:55,904
New York, Appleton-Century-Crofts, 1978.

114
00:15:55,904 --> 00:16:03,067
Beginning even before implantation, a remarkably effective period of myometrial quiescence is imposed.

115
00:16:03,067 --> 00:16:13,467
This phase 1 normally constitutes 95 percent of pregnancy and is characterized by uterine smooth muscle tranquility with maintenance of cervical structural integrity .

116
00:16:13,467 --> 00:16:24,579
All manner of molecular systems—neural, endocrine, paracrine, and autocrine— are likely called to implement and coordinate a state of relative uterine unresponsiveness.

117
00:16:24,579 --> 00:16:34,192
Moreover, a complementary “fail-safe” system that protects the uterus against agents that could perturb the tranquility of phase 1 also must be in place.

118
00:16:34,192 --> 00:16:36,904
Figure 21-5.

119
00:16:36,904 --> 00:16:41,417
The key factors thought to regulate the phases of human parturition.

120
00:16:41,417 --> 00:16:57,417
CRH = corticotropin-releasing hormone; hCG = human chorionic gonadotropin; PAF = platelet-activating factor; PGDH = prostaglandin dehydrogenase; SPA = surfactant protein A.

121
00:16:57,417 --> 00:17:05,442
During phase 1, the uterus must initiate extensive changes in its size and vascularity to accommodate fetal growth.

122
00:17:05,442 --> 00:17:16,067
Concurrently, myometrial cells undergo a phenotypic modification to a noncontractile state, and uterine muscle is rendered unresponsive to natural stimuli.

123
00:17:16,067 --> 00:17:25,779
Although this unresponsiveness continues until near the end of pregnancy, some lowintensity myometrial contractions are felt during the quiescent phase.

124
00:17:25,779 --> 00:17:34,317
These contractions do not normally cause cervical dilation, and they are common toward the end of pregnancy, especially in multiparas.

125
00:17:34,317 --> 00:17:38,842
They are referred to as Braxton Hicks contractions or false labor .

126
00:17:38,842 --> 00:18:03,792
The quiescence of phase 1 likely stems from: (1) actions of estrogen and progesterone via intracellular receptors, (2) myometrial-cell plasma membrane receptor–mediated increases in cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP), (3) generation of cyclic guanosine monophosphate, and (4) other systems, including modification of myometrial-cell ion channels.

127
00:18:03,792 --> 00:18:07,854
# Myometrial Relaxation and Contraction.

128
00:18:07,854 --> 00:18:18,567
The balance between myometrial relaxation and contraction is controlled by steroid- and peptide-hormone transcriptional regulation of key genes and their protein products .

129
00:18:18,567 --> 00:18:40,954
Quiescence is achieved in part by: (1) diminished intracellular crosstalk and reduced intracellular Ca2+ ([Ca2+]i) levels, (2) ion-channel regulation of cell membrane potential, (3) activation of the stress–unfolded protein response by the uterine endoplasmic reticulum, and (4) uterotonin degradation.

130
00:18:40,954 --> 00:18:58,554
In contrast, contractility results from: (1) enhanced interactions between the actin and myosin proteins, (2) heightened excitability of individual myometrial cells, and (3) promotion of intracellular crosstalk that allows synchronous contractions to develop.

131
00:18:58,554 --> 00:19:04,842
Actin–Myosin Interactions Actin and myosin proteins are essential to muscle contraction.

132
00:19:04,842 --> 00:19:09,854
For this, actin must be converted from a globular to a filamentous form.

133
00:19:09,854 --> 00:19:20,229
Indeed, a potential mechanism for maintenance of relaxation is the promotion of actin into a globular form rather than into fibrils, which are required for contraction.

134
00:19:20,229 --> 00:19:24,904
Moreover, actin must be attached to the cytoskeleton at focal

135
00:19:25,060 --> 00:19:28,810
points in the cell membrane to allow tension to develop.

136
00:19:28,760 --> 00:19:34,485
Actin must partner with myosin, which is composed of multiple light and heavy chains .

137
00:19:34,485 --> 00:19:41,110
This interaction is brought about by enzymatic phosphorylation of the 20-kDa light chain of myosin .

138
00:19:41,110 --> 00:19:48,747
This is catalyzed by the enzyme myosin light-chain kinase, which is activated by calcium (Ca2+).

139
00:19:48,747 --> 00:19:51,410
Figure 21-6.

140
00:19:51,410 --> 00:19:55,147
Uterine myocyte relaxation and contraction.

141
00:19:55,147 --> 00:20:03,685
A. Uterine relaxation is maintained by factors that increase myocyte cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) levels.

142
00:20:03,685 --> 00:20:14,122
This activates protein kinase A (PKA) to promote phosphodiesterase activity with dephosphorylation of myosin light-chain kinase (MLCK).

143
00:20:14,122 --> 00:20:22,035
Other processes serve to maintain actin in a globular form and thus to prevent the fibril formation necessary for contractions.

144
00:20:22,035 --> 00:20:26,610
B. Uterine contractions result from reversal of these sequences.

145
00:20:26,610 --> 00:20:34,197
Actin now assumes a fibrillar form, and calcium enters the cell to combine with calmodulin to form complexes.

146
00:20:34,197 --> 00:20:40,685
These complexes activate MLCK to bring about phosphorylation of the myosin light chains.

147
00:20:40,685 --> 00:20:48,447
This generates ATPase activity to cause sliding of myosin over the actin fibrils, which is a uterine contraction.

148
00:20:48,447 --> 00:21:15,622
AC = adenylyl cyclase; Ca2+ = calcium; DAG = diacylglycerol; Gs and Gα = G-receptor proteins; IP 3 = inositol triphosphate; LC20 = light chain 20; PIP 3 = phosphatidylinositol 3,4,5– triphosphate; PLC = phospholipase C; R-PKA = inactive protein kinase.

149
00:21:15,622 --> 00:21:18,897
Redrawn from Smith R: Parturition.

150
00:21:18,897 --> 00:21:26,947
N Engl J Med. 2007 Jan 18;356(3):271–283.

151
00:21:26,947 --> 00:21:35,922
Thus, logically, uterine relaxation ordinarily is promoted by conditions that lower concentrations of (Ca2+)i.

152
00:21:35,922 --> 00:21:43,872
In contrast, agents that prompt contraction act on myometrial cells to augment (Ca2+)i levels.

153
00:21:43,872 --> 00:21:52,960
Or, they allow an influx of extracellular calcium through ligand- or voltage-regulated calcium channels (see Fig. 21-6).

154
00:21:52,960 --> 00:22:02,397
For example, PGF2α and oxytocin bind their respective receptors during labor to open ligand-activated calcium channels.

155
00:22:02,397 --> 00:22:08,197
Activation of these receptors also releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.

156
00:22:08,197 --> 00:22:15,860
Additionally, greater localization of nonselective cation channels on the cell membrane promotes Ca2+ entry .

157
00:22:15,860 --> 00:22:20,535
The rise in (Ca2+)i levels is often transient.

158
00:22:20,535 --> 00:22:28,310
But, contractions can be prolonged by inhibition of myosin phosphatase, an enzyme that dephosphorylates myosin .

159
00:22:28,310 --> 00:22:39,235
Regulation of Membrane Potentials As just noted, myocyte excitability is regulated in part by changes in the electrochemical potential gradient across the plasma membrane.

160
00:22:39,235 --> 00:22:45,160
Before labor, myocytes maintain a relatively high interior electronegativity.

161
00:22:45,160 --> 00:22:53,297
Maintenance of a hyperpolarized membrane potential attenuates smooth muscle cell excitation and is regulated by ion channels.

162
00:22:53,297 --> 00:23:04,985
Numerous ion channels control sodium (Na+) and calcium (Ca2+) influx and potassium (K+) efflux to collectively regulate membrane potential.

163
00:23:04,985 --> 00:23:10,472
Sodium leak channels (NALCN) are important to normal uterine function .

164
00:23:10,472 --> 00:23:20,247
Another key regulator is the large-conductance voltage- and Ca2+-activated K+ channel (BKCa) (Pérez, 1993).

165
00:23:20,247 --> 00:23:30,822
The BKCa channel is abundantly expressed in the myometrium and plays dual promoting and opposing roles to maintain a balance between uterine quiescence and contractility.

166
00:23:30,822 --> 00:23:42,060
Opening the BKCa channel allows potassium to leave the cell to maintain interior electronegativity, thus preventing voltage-gated Ca2+ influx and contraction.

167
00:23:42,060 --> 00:23:47,285
Inhibition of the BKCa channel augments myometrial contractility.

168
00:23:47,285 --> 00:23:58,410
Regulation of uterine contractility from early to late gestation may result from temporal changes in expression of the BKCa channel and/or BKCa interacting partners .

169
00:23:58,410 --> 00:24:09,547
Myometrial Gap Junctions Cellular signals that control myometrial contraction and relaxation can be effectively transferred between cells through intercellular junctional channels.

170
00:24:09,547 --> 00:24:19,385
Communication is established between myocytes by gap junctions, which aid the passage of electrical or ionic coupling currents as well as metabolite coupling.

171
00:24:19,385 --> 00:24:24,422
The connexin family of proteins form cell-type specific gap junctions .

172
00:24:24,422 --> 00:24:31,122
Connexin-43 is expressed in the myometrium, and concentrations rise near labor onset.

173
00:24:31,122 --> 00:24:34,635
Six connexin subunits form a connexon,

174
00:24:34,804 --> 00:24:39,954
and two adjacent connexons establish a conduit between communicating cells.

175
00:24:39,904 --> 00:24:48,004
This permits exchange of small molecules that can be nutrients, waste, metabolites, second messengers, or ions.

176
00:24:48,004 --> 00:24:54,741
Optimal numbers and types of gap junctions are believed to be important for electrical myometrial synchrony.

177
00:24:54,741 --> 00:25:11,779
Progesterone promotes uterine quiescence in part by lowering expression of contraction-associated proteins (CAPs) and maintaining expression of anticontractile agents such as caspase 3 (Ingles, 2018; Kyathanahalli, 2015).

178
00:25:11,779 --> 00:25:18,954
CAPs include the oxytocin receptor, prostaglandin F receptor, and connexin43.

179
00:25:18,954 --> 00:25:37,154
Also, at the end of pregnancy, increased stretch, metabolism of progesterone by the enzyme 20α-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (20α-HSD), loss of progesterone-mediated repression of CAP genes, and greater estrogen dominance raise CAP levels.

180
00:25:37,154 --> 00:25:54,866
Integration of diverse regulatory pathways culminates in released inhibition of connexin-43 and oxytocin receptor levels to promote greater uterine contractility (Anamthathmakula, 2019; Mendelson, 2019; Peavey, 2021).

181
00:25:54,866 --> 00:26:04,254
G Protein–Coupled Receptors In addition to ion-channel linked receptors, G protein–coupled receptors regulate myocyte contractility.

182
00:26:04,254 --> 00:26:15,216
These receptors together with appropriate ligands may act with sex steroid hormones to maintain uterine quiescence (Price, 2000; Sanborn, 1998).

183
00:26:15,216 --> 00:26:25,591
Examples are the luteinizing hormone (LH) receptor and corticotropin-releasing hormone receptor 1 (CRHR1), both described in this section .

184
00:26:25,591 --> 00:26:32,891
Other G protein–coupled myometrial receptors, instead, are associated with uterine contractility.

185
00:26:32,891 --> 00:26:40,354
G protein– mediated activation of phospholipase C, which releases arachidonic acid, is one example.

186
00:26:40,354 --> 00:26:47,591
Ligands for the G protein–coupled receptors include numerous neuropeptides, hormones, and autacoids.

187
00:26:47,591 --> 00:26:55,354
Many of these are available to the myometrium during pregnancy in high concentration via endocrine or autocrine mechanisms.

188
00:26:55,354 --> 00:26:58,016
Figure 21-7.

189
00:26:58,016 --> 00:27:02,541
G protein–coupled receptor signal transduction pathways.

190
00:27:02,541 --> 00:27:15,841
A. Receptors coupled to heterotrimeric guanosine-triphosphate (GTP)-binding proteins (G proteins) are integral transmembrane proteins that transduce extracellular signals to the cell interior.

191
00:27:15,841 --> 00:27:23,129
G protein–coupled receptors exhibit a common structural motif consisting of seven membrane-spanning regions.

192
00:27:23,129 --> 00:27:31,016
B. Receptor occupation promotes interaction between the receptor and the G protein on the interior surface of the membrane.

193
00:27:31,016 --> 00:27:42,416
An exchange of guanosine diphosphate (GDP) for GTP on the G protein α subunit and dissociation of the α subunit from the βγ heterodimer occurs.

194
00:27:42,416 --> 00:28:00,416
Depending on its isoform, the GTP-α subunit complex mediates intracellular signaling either indirectly by acting on effector molecules such as adenylyl cyclase (AC) or phospholipase C (PLC), or directly by regulating ion channel or kinase function.

195
00:28:00,416 --> 00:28:09,766
cAMP = cyclic adenosine monophosphate; DAG = diacylglycerol; IP 3 = inositol triphosphate.

196
00:28:09,766 --> 00:28:17,079
LH and human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) hormones share the same LH-hCG receptor.

197
00:28:17,079 --> 00:28:26,166
This G protein–coupled receptor is found in myometrial smooth muscle and blood vessels, and levels during pregnancy are greater before labor .

198
00:28:26,166 --> 00:28:33,466
hCG acts to activate adenylyl cyclase by way of a plasma membrane receptor Gαs-linked system.

199
00:28:33,466 --> 00:28:44,791
This lessens contraction frequency and force and lowers the number of tissue-specific myometrial cell gap junctions (Ambrus, 1994; Eta, 1994).

200
00:28:44,791 --> 00:28:51,116
Thus, high circulating levels of hCG may be one mechanism of uterine quiescence.

201
00:28:51,116 --> 00:28:57,454
β-Adrenergic receptors mediate Gαs-stimulated myometrial cell relaxation.

202
00:28:57,454 --> 00:29:05,016
Agents binding to these receptors have been used for tocolysis of preterm labor and include ritodrine and terbutaline .

203
00:29:05,016 --> 00:29:11,954
The rate-limiting factor is likely the number of receptors expressed and the level of adenylyl cyclase expression.

204
00:29:11,954 --> 00:29:18,704
Prostaglandin E2 mediates its diverse cellular effects through four G protein–coupled receptors.

205
00:29:18,704 --> 00:29:32,816
Specifically, prostaglandin E receptors 1 through 4 (EP1-EP4) are expressed in the myometrium during pregnancy and with labor onset (Astle, 2005; Leonhardt, 2003).

206
00:29:32,816 --> 00:29:39,141
EP2 and EP4 act through Gαs to maintain myometrial cell quiescence but

207
00:29:39,292 --> 00:29:44,754
switch to a Gαq/11 calciumactivating pathway during labor .

208
00:29:44,704 --> 00:29:53,104
EP1 and EP3 receptors act through Gαq and Gαi to augment intracellular Ca2+ and contractility.

209
00:29:53,104 --> 00:30:02,154
The peptide hormone relaxin binds to the G protein–coupled receptor named relaxin family peptide receptor 1 (RXFP1).

210
00:30:02,154 --> 00:30:15,079
Binding activates adenylyl cyclase, which in turn prevents increased intracellular Ca2+, and thus promotes uterine quiescence (Downing, 1993; Meera, 1995).

211
00:30:15,079 --> 00:30:25,917
The H1 relaxin gene is primarily expressed in the decidua, trophoblast, and prostate, whereas the H2 gene is primarily expressed in the corpus luteum.

212
00:30:25,917 --> 00:30:33,154
Relaxin plasma levels peak at approximately 1 ng/mL between 8 and 12 weeks’ gestation.

213
00:30:33,154 --> 00:30:36,242
Thereafter, they decline until term.

214
00:30:36,242 --> 00:30:42,979
Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) is synthesized in the placenta and hypothalamus.

215
00:30:42,979 --> 00:31:01,454
Discussed on page 408, CRH plasma levels rise dramatically during the final 6 to 8 weeks of normal pregnancy and are implicated in mechanisms that control the timing of human parturition (Smith, 2007; Wadhwa, 1998; Wang, 2019).

216
00:31:01,454 --> 00:31:09,679
CRH appears to promote myometrial quiescence during most of pregnancy but then aids contractions with parturition onset.

217
00:31:09,679 --> 00:31:18,004
Studies suggest that these opposing actions are achieved by differential actions of CRH via its receptor CRHR1.

218
00:31:18,004 --> 00:31:27,642
In nonlaboring myometrium at term, the interaction of CRH with CRHR1 activates the Gsadenylate cyclase-cAMP signaling pathway.

219
00:31:27,642 --> 00:31:36,342
This results in inhibition of inositol triphosphate (IP3) and stabilization of (Ca2+)i levels .

220
00:31:36,342 --> 00:31:50,879
However, in term laboring myometrium, (Ca2+)i concentrations are augmented by CRH activation of G proteins Gq and Gi and prompts stimulation of IP3 production and greater contractility.

221
00:31:50,879 --> 00:31:58,279
Cyclic Guanosine Monophosphate As noted, cAMP is an important mediator of myometrial relaxation.

222
00:31:58,279 --> 00:32:06,704
However, activation of guanylyl cyclase raises intracellular cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP) levels.

223
00:32:06,704 --> 00:32:10,229
This also promotes smooth muscle relaxation .

224
00:32:10,229 --> 00:32:25,692
Intracellular cGMP levels are increased in the pregnant myometrium and can be stimulated by atrial natriuretic peptide, brain natriuretic peptide, and nitric oxide (Guerra, 2019; Telfer, 2001).

225
00:32:25,692 --> 00:32:30,579
All of these factors and their receptors are expressed in the pregnant uterus.

226
00:32:30,579 --> 00:32:41,192
Accelerated Uterotonin Degradation The activity of enzymes that degrade or inactivate endogenously produced uterotonins also is strikingly increased in phase 1.

227
00:32:41,192 --> 00:33:04,654
Pairs of some degrading enzymes and their respective targets include PGDH and prostaglandins; enkephalinase and endothelins; oxytocinase and oxytocin; diamine oxidase and histamine; catechol O-methyltransferase and catecholamines; angiotensinases and angiotensin II; and platelet-activating factor (PAF) and PAF acetylhydrolase.

228
00:33:04,654 --> 00:33:08,904
Levels of several of these enzymes decline late in gestation .

229
00:33:08,904 --> 00:33:11,142
# Decidua.

230
00:33:11,142 --> 00:33:20,392
To ensure uterine quiescence, the synthesis in the decidua of prostaglandins, in particular PGF2α, is markedly suppressed.

231
00:33:20,392 --> 00:33:29,117
Suppression of prostaglandin production here persists throughout most of pregnancy, and suppression withdrawal is a prerequisite for parturition .

232
00:33:29,117 --> 00:33:35,592
The mother and fetus come in direct contact at the decidua basalis and decidua parietalis.

233
00:33:35,592 --> 00:33:55,629
At this interface, myeloid and lymphoid immune cells such as natural killer cells, macrophages, dendritic cells, and T cells undergo dynamic changes in number and phenotype to achieve a balanced microenvironment (Ander, 2019; Solano, 2019; van der Zwan, 2020).

234
00:33:55,629 --> 00:34:05,392
This is a critical site for immune tolerance in phase 1 and for decidual activation in phase 2 of parturition (see Fig. 21-5).

235
00:34:05,392 --> 00:34:14,917
In phase 1 of parturition, these cells, in collaboration with decidual cells, promote an environment of immune tolerance to protect the fetus.

236
00:34:14,917 --> 00:34:25,417
Immune cells with innate and adaptive immune responses support vascular and tissue remodeling, immune surveillance and defense, and trophoblast invasion in phase 1 .

237
00:34:25,417 --> 00:34:27,942
Figure 21-8.

238
00:34:27,942 --> 00:34:32,392
Immune tolerance and activation at the maternal fetal interface.

239
00:34:32,392 --> 00:34:40,467
In the antepartum period, dNK cells are abundant and their maturation is promoted by release of uterine IL-15.

240
00:34:40,467 --> 00:34:46,867
These cells promote decidual remodeling, blastocyst implantation, and EVT invasion.

241
00:34:46,867 --> 00:34:48,192
The

242
00:34:48,340 --> 00:34:56,665
cytotoxicity of dNK cells is regulated by the interaction of its KIR receptor with HLA-G on EVTs.

243
00:34:56,615 --> 00:35:05,127
Decidual macrophages— primary antigen-presenting cells—produce the enzyme IDO, which limits T cell activation.

244
00:35:05,127 --> 00:35:15,502
Changes in the intrapartum period are less understood, but the number of dNK cells decline and decidual macrophages are activated to a proinflammatory phenotype.

245
00:35:15,502 --> 00:35:33,365
dNK = decidual natural killer; EVT = extravillous trophoblasts; HLA = human leukocyte antigen; IL = interleukin; IDO = indoleamine 2,3dioxygenase; KIR = killer-cell immunoglobulin-like receptors.

246
00:35:33,365 --> 00:35:41,165
During phase 2, decidual stromal cells and fetal membranes undergo an aging process termed cellular senescence.

247
00:35:41,165 --> 00:35:52,990
Noninflammatory signals precipitate senescence, which in turn leads to synthesis of proinflammatory cytokines, augmented prostaglandin production, and increased protease expression.

248
00:35:52,990 --> 00:35:57,065
These proteins and hormones break down fetal membranes.

249
00:35:57,065 --> 00:35:59,677
# Cervical Softening.

250
00:35:59,677 --> 00:36:06,240
The initial stage of cervical remodeling—termed softening—begins in phase 1 of parturition.

251
00:36:06,240 --> 00:36:12,390
It is characterized by greater tissue compliance, yet the cervix remains firm and unyielding.

252
00:36:12,390 --> 00:36:23,752
Hegar (1895) first described palpable softening of the lower uterine segment at 4 to 6 weeks’ gestation, and this sign was once used to diagnose pregnancy.

253
00:36:23,752 --> 00:36:31,415
Clinically, the maintenance of cervical anatomical and structural integrity is essential for pregnancy to continue to term.

254
00:36:31,415 --> 00:36:37,627
Preterm cervical dilation, structural insufficiency, or both may forecast delivery.

255
00:36:37,627 --> 00:36:40,615
# Cervical Connective Tissue.

256
00:36:40,615 --> 00:36:59,965
Cervical softening is an active molecular process that balances tissue competence against slow, progressive compositional and structural changes in the extracellular matrix (ECM) to increase compliance (Myers, 2015; Nallasamy, 2017; Vink, 2018).

257
00:36:59,965 --> 00:37:11,165
Constituents of the ECM include type I and III fibrillar collagens, matricellular proteins, glycosaminoglycans, proteoglycans, and elastic fibers.

258
00:37:11,165 --> 00:37:21,602
Key to matrix changes, collagen, which is the main structural protein in the cervix, undergoes conformational changes that alter tissue stiffness and flexibility .

259
00:37:21,602 --> 00:37:30,252
Specifically, collagen processing and the number or type of stable covalent cross-links between collagen’s triple helices are altered.

260
00:37:30,252 --> 00:37:45,277
Beginning in early pregnancy, mature cross-links between newly synthesized collagen monomers are reduced due to diminished expression and activity of the cross-link-forming enzymes (Akins, 2011; Yoshida, 2014).

261
00:37:45,277 --> 00:37:50,890
These enzymes are lysyl hydroxylase and lysyl oxidase.

262
00:37:50,890 --> 00:37:58,715
Gradual turnover of collagen during pregnancy replaces mature cross-linked collagen fibrils with poorly linked fibrils.

263
00:37:58,715 --> 00:38:02,227
The result is a collagen with reduced stiffness.

264
00:38:02,227 --> 00:38:12,527
The collagen-binding proteoglycans— decorin and biglycan—ensure the new, poorly cross-linked collagen is appropriately assembled and deposited in the ECM.

265
00:38:12,527 --> 00:38:29,815
Data from human and mouse studies support the theory that a balance in the synthesis and breakdown of collagen, rather than loss of collagen, achieves cervical remodeling (Akins, 2011; Myers, 2008; Read, 2007; Yoshida, 2014).

266
00:38:29,815 --> 00:38:32,415
Figure 21-9.

267
00:38:32,415 --> 00:38:39,252
Mouse studies identify molecular changes in collagen structure and function during cervical remodeling.

268
00:38:39,252 --> 00:38:45,802
These changes are regulated by steroid hormones and result in a gradual loss of tissue stiffness.

269
00:38:45,802 --> 00:38:59,177
During the softening phase, the decline in lysyl oxidase activity in conjunction with high collagen turnover results in the replacement of strong cross-linked collagen fibers with weakly crosslinked collagen fibers.

270
00:38:59,177 --> 00:39:07,902
Second harmonic generation images in the mouse and human cervix show the corresponding gradual decline in mechanical function of the cervix.

271
00:39:07,902 --> 00:39:19,965
Compiled from Akins, 2010, 2011; Ozasa, 1981; Read, 2007; Yoshida, 2014; Zhang, 2012.

272
00:39:19,965 --> 00:39:32,852
Clinical evidence for the importance of matrix changes to cervical softening is supported by in vivo mechanical evaluation of the cervix (Badir, 2013; Parra-Saavedra, 2011).

273
00:39:32,852 --> 00:39:48,177
The prevalence of cervical insufficiency is also higher in those with inherited defects in the synthesis or assembly of collagen or elastic fibers (Anum, 2009; Hermanns-Le, 2005; Volozonoka, 2020).

274
00:39:48,177 --> 00:39:50,140
Examples are

275
00:39:50,308 --> 00:39:56,933
Ehlers-Danlos and Marfan syndromes, discussed in Chapter 62 (p. 1121).

276
00:39:56,883 --> 00:40:05,320
Concurrent with matrix remodeling in the softening period, genes involved in cervical dilation and parturition are actively repressed .

277
00:40:05,320 --> 00:40:14,170
PHASE 2: PREPARATION FOR LABOR To prepare for labor, the myometrial tranquility of phase 1 of parturition must be suspended.

278
00:40:14,170 --> 00:40:23,258
This so-called uterine awakening or activation in phase 2 of parturition is a progression of uterine changes during the last few weeks of pregnancy.

279
00:40:23,258 --> 00:40:29,870
Importantly, shifting events associated with phase 2 can cause either preterm or delayed labor.

280
00:40:29,870 --> 00:40:32,658
# Progesterone Withdrawal.

281
00:40:32,658 --> 00:40:38,045
Key factors in uterine activation are depicted in Figure 21-5.

282
00:40:38,045 --> 00:40:46,620
In species that exhibit progesterone withdrawal, parturition progression to labor can be blocked by administering progesterone to the mother.

283
00:40:46,620 --> 00:40:57,020
Whether progesterone administration in the absence of classic progesterone withdrawal can delay the timely onset of parturition or prevent preterm labor remains unclear.

284
00:40:57,020 --> 00:41:05,933
The possibility that progesterone may prevent preterm labor has been studied, but its use in preventing preterm birth continues to be debated .

285
00:41:05,933 --> 00:41:11,820
Classic progesterone withdrawal from decreased secretion does not occur in human parturition.

286
00:41:11,820 --> 00:41:25,608
However, a mechanism for progesterone inactivation, whereby the myometrium and cervix become refractory to progesterone’s inhibitory actions, is supported by studies using progesteronereceptor antagonists.

287
00:41:25,608 --> 00:41:32,058
Mifepristone is a classic steroid antagonist, acting at the level of the progesterone receptor.

288
00:41:32,058 --> 00:41:48,433
Although less effective in inducing abortion or labor later in pregnancy, mifepristone appears to have some effect on cervical ripening and on increasing myometrial sensitivity to uterotonins (Berkane, 2005; Chwalisz, 1994a).

289
00:41:48,433 --> 00:41:56,195
The diverse mechanisms by which functional progesterone withdrawal or antagonism is achieved is an active area of research.

290
00:41:56,195 --> 00:42:30,758
These include: (1) changes in the relative expression of the nuclear progesterone-receptor (PR) isoforms, (2) differential interaction of PR isoforms A and B with enhancers and inhibitors of gene expression, (3) altered PR activity through changes in the expression of coactivators or corepressors of receptor function, (4) local progesterone inactivation by steroid-metabolizing enzymes or synthesis of a natural antagonist, and (5) microRNA regulation of progesteronemetabolizing enzymes and

291
00:42:30,758 --> 00:42:50,508
transcription factors that modulate uterine quiescence (Chen, 2017; Condon, 2003; Mahendroo, 1999; Mesiano, 2002; Nadeem, 2016; Peavey, 2021; Renthal, 2010; Williams, 2012).

292
00:42:50,508 --> 00:42:55,870
Thus, multiple pathways exist to create functional progesterone withdrawal.

293
00:42:55,870 --> 00:42:58,808
# Myometrial Changes.

294
00:42:58,808 --> 00:43:03,670
Phase 2 changes in the myometrium prepare it for labor contractions.

295
00:43:03,670 --> 00:43:20,045
Changes result from a shift in the expression of proteins that control uterine quiescence to an expression of CAPs, described earlier (p. 403) (Mendelson, 2019; Renthal, 2015; Stanfield, 2019).

296
00:43:20,045 --> 00:43:24,595
These CAPs increase uterine responsiveness to uterotonins.

297
00:43:24,595 --> 00:43:30,558
Another critical change in phase 2 is formation of the lower uterine segment from the isthmus.

298
00:43:30,558 --> 00:43:36,833
With this development, the fetal head often descends to or even through the pelvic inlet—lightening.

299
00:43:36,833 --> 00:43:43,208
The abdomen commonly undergoes a shape change, sometimes described by women as “the baby dropped.

300
00:43:43,208 --> 00:43:53,108
It is also likely that the lower segment myometrium is unique from that in the upper uterine segment, and leads to distinct roles for each near term and during labor.

301
00:43:53,108 --> 00:44:09,533
This is supported by human studies that demonstrate differential expression of prostaglandin receptors and CAPs within the upper- and lower-segment myometrial regions (Astle, 2005; Blanks, 2003; Sparey, 1999).

302
00:44:09,533 --> 00:44:22,333
Near term, elevated expression of the HoxA13 gene in the lower myometrial segment compared with the upper segment also induces CAP expression and regionalized contractility of the lower segment .

303
00:44:22,333 --> 00:44:33,020
Oxytocin Receptors Because of its long-standing application for labor induction, it seemed logical that oxytocin must play a central role in spontaneous human labor.

304
00:44:33,020 --> 00:44:39,983
Myometrial oxytocin receptor levels do rise during phase 2 of parturition, and the level of

305
00:44:40,132 --> 00:44:47,682
oxytocin receptor mRNA in human myometrium at term is greater than that found in preterm myometrium .

306
00:44:47,632 --> 00:44:57,494
However, it is unclear whether oxytocin plays a role in the early phases of uterine activation or whether its sole function is in the expulsive phase of labor.

307
00:44:57,494 --> 00:45:04,369
Most studies of regulation of myometrial oxytocin receptor synthesis have been performed in rodents.

308
00:45:04,369 --> 00:45:09,832
Disruption of the oxytocin receptor gene in the mouse does not affect parturition.

309
00:45:09,832 --> 00:45:16,744
This suggests that, at least in this species, multiple systems likely ensure that parturition occurs.

310
00:45:16,744 --> 00:45:22,344
Progesterone inhibits and estradiol induces oxytocin receptor expression.

311
00:45:22,344 --> 00:45:29,594
Through interaction with PR-B, progesterone regulates numerous genes in the oxytocin signaling pathway .

312
00:45:29,594 --> 00:45:36,219
Progesterone may also act within the myometrial cell to enhance oxytocin receptor degradation .

313
00:45:36,219 --> 00:45:46,269
These data indicate that one of the mechanisms whereby progesterone maintains uterine quiescence is through inhibition of a myometrial oxytocin signaling pathway.

314
00:45:46,269 --> 00:45:48,769
# Cervical Ripening.

315
00:45:48,769 --> 00:45:55,169
Before contractions begin, the cervix must shift from a state of competence to one of compliance.

316
00:45:55,169 --> 00:46:01,232
This eventually leads to the cervix yielding and dilating from forceful uterine contractions.

317
00:46:01,232 --> 00:46:08,469
Cervical modifications during phase 2 principally involve connective tissue changes—termed cervical ripening.

318
00:46:08,469 --> 00:46:14,169
The transition from the softening to the ripening phase begins weeks or days before labor.

319
00:46:14,169 --> 00:46:19,319
The understanding of this cervical matrix transformation remains incomplete.

320
00:46:19,319 --> 00:46:28,094
However, it is clear that levels of glycosaminoglycans, which are large linear polysaccharides, are uniquely increased in phase 2.

321
00:46:28,094 --> 00:46:41,732
Glycosaminoglycans and Proteoglycans Hyaluronan is a high-molecular-weight polysaccharide that functions alone, but most other glycosaminoglycans (GAGs) complex with proteins to form proteoglycans.

322
00:46:41,732 --> 00:46:54,244
Hyaluronan is a hydrophilic, space-filling molecule, and thus greater hyaluronan production during cervical ripening is thought to increase viscoelasticity, hydration, and matrix disorganization.

323
00:46:54,244 --> 00:47:07,607
Hyaluronan synthesis is carried out by hyaluronan synthase isoenzymes, and expression of these enzymes is elevated in the cervix during ripening (Akgul, 2012; Straach, 2005).

324
00:47:07,607 --> 00:47:17,982
Inflammatory Changes In phase 2, resident immune cells are localized to the cervical stroma, although a functional role for them in this phase has not been demonstrated.

325
00:47:17,982 --> 00:47:27,582
For example, studies of gene expression patterns at term both before and after cervical ripening show little rise in proinflammatory gene expression.

326
00:47:27,582 --> 00:47:38,832
However, once labor is underway, activation of neutrophils, proinflammatory M1 macrophages, and tissue repair M2 macrophages in the cervix is augmented.

327
00:47:38,832 --> 00:47:51,644
Moreover, proinflammatory and immunosuppressive gene expression in the cervix increases markedly after delivery (Bollapragada, 2009; Hassan, 2006, 2009).

328
00:47:51,644 --> 00:47:57,769
This suggests a role for inflammatory cells in postpartum cervical remodeling and repair .

329
00:47:57,769 --> 00:48:03,169
Induction of Cervical Ripening No therapies prevent premature cervical ripening.

330
00:48:03,169 --> 00:48:12,644
In contrast, treatment to promote cervical ripening for labor induction includes direct application of PGE2 and PGF2α .

331
00:48:12,644 --> 00:48:21,944
Prostaglandins likely modify ECM structure to aid ripening, although their role in the normal physiology of cervical ripening remains unclear.

332
00:48:21,944 --> 00:48:29,957
In some nonhuman species, events that allow cervical ripening are induced by dropping serum progesterone concentrations.

333
00:48:29,957 --> 00:48:35,782
And in humans, administration of progesterone antagonists causes cervical ripening.

334
00:48:35,782 --> 00:48:48,407
Endocervical Epithelia In addition to matrix changes during pregnancy, endocervical epithelial cells proliferate such that endocervical glands account for a significant percentage of cervical mass.

335
00:48:48,407 --> 00:48:54,919
The endocervical canal is lined with mucus-secreting columnar and stratified squamous epithelia.

336
00:48:54,919 --> 00:49:07,769
These cells form both a mucosal barrier and a tight junctional barrier that protect against microbial invasion (Akgul, 2014; Blaskewicz, 2011; Timmons, 2007).

337
00:49:07,769 --> 00:49:19,807
The mucosal epithelium recognizes and deters pathogen invasion via expression of toll-like receptors that identify pathogens and via antimicrobial peptides and protease inhibitors.

338
00:49:19,807 --> 00:49:21,357
These

339
00:49:21,508 --> 00:49:29,270
epithelia also express signals to underlying immune cells when a pathogenic challenge exceeds their protective capacity .

340
00:49:29,220 --> 00:49:32,808
# Fetal Contributions to Parturition.

341
00:49:32,808 --> 00:49:41,870
It is intriguing to envision that the mature human fetus provides the signal to initiate parturition, and evidence for fetal signaling is mounting .

342
00:49:41,870 --> 00:49:49,058
The fetus may give signals through blood-borne agents that act on the placenta or through secretion into the amnionic fluid.

343
00:49:49,058 --> 00:49:55,870
Uterine Stretch Fetal growth is an important component of uterine activation in phase 2 of parturition.

344
00:49:55,870 --> 00:50:01,733
With uterine activation, stretch is required for induction of specific CAPs.

345
00:50:01,733 --> 00:50:08,008
Namely, stretch increases expression of connexin-43 and oxytocin receptors.

346
00:50:08,008 --> 00:50:16,558
Levels of gastrin-releasing peptide, a stimulatory agonist for smooth muscle, also are augmented by stretch in the myometrium .

347
00:50:16,558 --> 00:50:25,833
Clinical clues for a role of stretch come from the observation that multifetal pregnancies carry a much greater risk for preterm labor than singleton ones.

348
00:50:25,833 --> 00:50:31,370
Preterm labor is also more common in pregnancies complicated by hydramnios.

349
00:50:31,370 --> 00:50:38,658
Although the mechanisms causing preterm birth in these two are debated, a role for uterine stretch must be considered.

350
00:50:38,658 --> 00:50:44,220
Cell signaling systems are influenced by stretch to regulate the myometrial cell.

351
00:50:44,220 --> 00:51:01,883
This process—mechanotransduction—may include activation of cell-surface receptors or ion channels, transmission of signals through ECM, or release of autocrine molecules that act directly on myometrium (Shynlova, 2007; Young, 2011).

352
00:51:01,883 --> 00:51:11,120
Fetal Endocrine Cascades The ability of the fetus to provide endocrine signals that initiate parturition has been demonstrated in several species.

353
00:51:11,120 --> 00:51:16,695
However, evidence suggests that it is not regulated in the same manner in humans.

354
00:51:16,695 --> 00:51:25,270
That said, the human fetal hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal-placental axis is considered a critical component of normal parturition.

355
00:51:25,270 --> 00:51:34,645
Moreover, premature activation of this axis is considered to prompt many cases of preterm labor (Challis, 2000, 2001).

356
00:51:34,645 --> 00:51:45,658
Steroid products of the human fetal adrenal gland are believed to have effects on the placenta and membranes that eventually transform the myometrium from a quiescent to a contractile state.

357
00:51:45,658 --> 00:52:00,020
A key component in the human may be the unique ability of the placenta to produce large amounts of CRH that is identical to maternal and fetal hypothalamic CRH (Grino, 1987; Saijonmaa, 1988) .

358
00:52:00,020 --> 00:52:10,208
However, unlike hypothalamic CRH, which is under glucocorticoid negative feedback, cortisol instead stimulates placental CRH production.

359
00:52:10,208 --> 00:52:17,008
This ability makes it possible to create a feed-forward endocrine cascade that does not end until delivery.

360
00:52:17,008 --> 00:52:19,570
Figure 21-10.

361
00:52:19,570 --> 00:52:23,370
The placental–fetal adrenal endocrine cascade.

362
00:52:23,370 --> 00:52:35,345
In late gestation, placental corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) stimulates fetal adrenal production of dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEA-S) and cortisol.

363
00:52:35,345 --> 00:52:44,433
The latter stimulates production of placental CRH, which leads to a feed-forward cascade that enhances adrenal steroid hormone production.

364
00:52:44,433 --> 00:52:48,320
ACTH = adrenocorticotropic hormone.

365
00:52:48,320 --> 00:52:54,858
Maternal plasma CRH levels are low in the first trimester and rise from midgestation to term.

366
00:52:54,858 --> 00:53:07,408
In the last 12 weeks, CRH plasma levels rise exponentially, peak during labor, and then fall precipitously after delivery (Frim, 1988; Sasaki, 1987).

367
00:53:07,408 --> 00:53:13,083
Amnionic fluid CRH concentrations similarly increase in late gestation.

368
00:53:13,083 --> 00:53:19,495
CRH is the only trophic hormone-releasing factor to have a specific serum binding protein.

369
00:53:19,495 --> 00:53:29,070
During most of pregnancy, CRHbinding protein (CRH-BP) binds most maternal circulating CRH, and this inactivates it .

370
00:53:29,070 --> 00:53:44,883
During later pregnancy, however, CRH-BP levels in both maternal plasma and amnionic fluid decline, leading to markedly greater levels of bioavailable CRH (Perkins, 1995; Petraglia, 1997).

371
00:53:44,883 --> 00:54:02,495
In pregnancies in which the fetus can be considered “stressed” from various complications, concentrations of CRH in fetal plasma, amnionic fluid, and maternal plasma are greater than those seen in normal gestation (Berkowitz, 1996; McGrath, 2002).

372
00:54:02,495 --> 00:54:07,583
The placenta is the likely source of this elevated CRH concentration.

373
00:54:07,583 --> 00:54:12,520
For example, placental CRH content is fourfold higher in

374
00:54:12,676 --> 00:54:17,538
placentas from women with preeclampsia than in those from normal pregnancies .

375
00:54:17,488 --> 00:54:22,701
Placental CRH is thought to play several roles in parturition regulation.

376
00:54:22,701 --> 00:54:29,776
It may enhance fetal cortisol production to provide positive feedback so that the placenta produces more CRH.

377
00:54:29,776 --> 00:54:44,276
Late in pregnancy—phase 2 or 3 of parturition—modification in the CRH receptor favors a switch from cAMP formation instead to increased myometrial cell calcium levels via protein kinase C activation .

378
00:54:44,276 --> 00:54:51,613
Oxytocin acts to attenuate CRH-stimulated accumulation of cAMP in myometrial tissue.

379
00:54:51,613 --> 00:54:58,251
CRH acts to augment myometrial contractile force in response to PGF2α .

380
00:54:58,251 --> 00:55:07,451
Last, CRH stimulates fetal adrenal C19-steroid synthesis, thereby increasing substrate for placental aromatization.

381
00:55:07,451 --> 00:55:14,013
Some have proposed that the rising CRH level at the end of gestation reflects a fetal-placental clock .

382
00:55:14,013 --> 00:55:28,351
CRH concentrations vary greatly among women, and the rate of rise in maternal CRH levels is a more accurate predictor of pregnancy outcome than is a single measurement (Leung, 2001; McGrath, 2002).

383
00:55:28,351 --> 00:55:37,201
In this regard, the placenta and fetus, through endocrinological events, influence parturition timing at the end of normal gestation.

384
00:55:37,201 --> 00:55:46,876
Fetal Lung Surfactant and Platelet-activating Factor Surfactant protein A (SP-A) produced by the fetal lung is required for lung maturation.

385
00:55:46,876 --> 00:56:02,938
SP-A is expressed by the human amnion and decidua, is present in the amnionic fluid, and prompts signaling pathways in human myometrial cells (Garcia-Verdugo, 2008; Lee, 2010; Snegovskikh, 2011).

386
00:56:02,938 --> 00:56:11,251
The exact mechanisms by which SP-A activates myometrial contractility in women, however, remain to be clarified.

387
00:56:11,251 --> 00:56:14,863
One mode may be its effects on prostaglandins.

388
00:56:14,863 --> 00:56:24,176
Namely, SP-A selectively inhibits PGF2α in the term decidua, but SP-A levels drop in the amnionic fluid at term .

389
00:56:24,176 --> 00:56:33,688
The fetal lung also makes the uterotonic agent platelet-activating factor (Frenkel, 1996; Toyoshima, 1995).

390
00:56:33,688 --> 00:56:39,288
This factor and SP-A play a role in fetal– maternal signaling for parturition .

391
00:56:39,288 --> 00:56:46,013
Fetal Membrane Senescence Toward the end of pregnancy, fetal membranes undergo cellular senescence .

392
00:56:46,013 --> 00:56:58,888
In human and animal fetal membranes, stretch and oxidative stress induce senescent fetal membrane to manifest a form of sterile inflammation termed senescent-associated secretory phenotype (SASP).

393
00:56:58,888 --> 00:57:08,951
This in turn propagates inflammatory signals that further weaken the fetal membrane and activate signals in the decidua and myometrium to initiate parturition.

394
00:57:08,951 --> 00:57:18,076
Thus, as the functional necessity of fetal membranes declines at term, they are able to promote signals that contribute to parturition initiation.

395
00:57:18,076 --> 00:57:27,888
Fetal Anomalies and Delayed Parturition Some evidence shows that pregnancies with markedly diminished estrogen production may be associated with prolonged gestation.

396
00:57:27,888 --> 00:57:35,438
These include women with inherited placental sulfatase deficiency and fetal anencephaly with adrenal hypoplasia.

397
00:57:35,438 --> 00:57:45,201
The broad range of gestational length seen with these disorders, however, calls into question the exact role of estrogen in human parturition initiation.

398
00:57:45,201 --> 00:57:53,188
Some brain anomalies of the fetal calf, fetal lamb, and sometimes the human fetus delay the normal timing of parturition.

399
00:57:53,188 --> 00:58:03,976
In particular, the association between fetal anencephaly and prolonged gestation is well described (Rea, 1898; Malpas, 1933).

400
00:58:03,976 --> 00:58:11,101
Malpas concluded that the prolonged gestation was attributable to anomalous fetal brain-pituitary-adrenal function.

401
00:58:11,101 --> 00:58:21,138
Indeed, the adrenal glands of the anencephalic fetus are very small and, at term, may be only 5 to 10 percent as large as those of a normal fetus.

402
00:58:21,138 --> 00:58:30,638
This is caused by developmental failure of the fetal zone that normally accounts for most of fetal adrenal mass and production of C19-steroid hormones .

403
00:58:30,638 --> 00:58:39,213
Such pregnancies are associated with delayed labor and suggest that the fetal adrenal glands are important for the timely onset of parturition.

404
00:58:39,213 --> 00:58:49,138
Other fetal abnormalities that prevent or severely reduce the entry of fetal urine or lung secretions into amnionic fluid do not prolong human pregnancy.

405
00:58:49,138 --> 00:58:54,451
Examples are renal agenesis and pulmonary hypoplasia, respectively.

406
00:58:54,451 --> 00:58:58,001
Thus, a fetal signal through the paracrine arm

407
00:58:58,156 --> 00:59:04,831
of the fetal–maternal communication system does not appear to be mandated for parturition initiation.

408
00:59:04,781 --> 00:59:14,306
PHASE 3: LABOR This phase is synonymous with active labor, which is customarily divided into three stages (see Figure 21-4).

409
00:59:14,306 --> 00:59:24,706
The first stage begins when spaced uterine contractions of sufficient frequency, intensity, and duration are attained to bring about cervical thinning, termed effacement.

410
00:59:24,706 --> 00:59:32,118
Several uterotonins may be important to the success of this stage of active labor (see Fig. 21-5).

411
00:59:32,118 --> 00:59:37,331
These have been shown to stimulate smooth muscle contraction through G-protein coupling.

412
00:59:37,331 --> 00:59:45,368
This labor stage ends when the cervix is fully dilated—about 10 cm—to allow passage of the term-sized fetus.

413
00:59:45,368 --> 00:59:51,168
The first stage of labor, therefore, is the stage of cervical effacement and dilation.

414
00:59:51,168 --> 00:59:56,593
The second stage begins when cervical dilation is complete and ends with delivery.

415
00:59:56,593 --> 01:00:01,218
Thus, the second stage of labor is the stage of fetal expulsion.

416
01:00:01,218 --> 01:00:08,318
Last, the third stage begins immediately after delivery of the fetus and ends with the delivery of the placenta.

417
01:00:08,318 --> 01:00:13,218
This stage of labor is the stage of placental separation and expulsion.

418
01:00:13,218 --> 01:00:17,218
# First Stage: Clinical Onset of Labor.

419
01:00:17,218 --> 01:00:24,418
Uterine Labor Contractions In some women, forceful uterine contractions that effect delivery begin suddenly.

420
01:00:24,418 --> 01:00:32,318
In others, labor initiation is heralded by spontaneous release of a small amount of blood-tinged mucus from the vagina.

421
01:00:32,318 --> 01:00:39,956
This extrusion of the mucus plug that had previously filled the cervical canal during pregnancy is referred to as “bloody show.

422
01:00:39,956 --> 01:00:46,181
Its passage indicates that labor is already in progress or likely will ensue in hours to days.

423
01:00:46,181 --> 01:00:53,218
Unique among physiological muscular contractions, those of uterine smooth muscle during labor are painful.

424
01:00:53,218 --> 01:01:13,843
Suggested causes are: (1) hypoxia of the contracted myometrium—such as that with angina pectoris; (2) compression of nerve ganglia in the cervix and lower uterus by contracted interlocking muscle bundles; (3) cervical stretching during dilation; and (4) stretching of the peritoneum overlying the fundus.

425
01:01:13,843 --> 01:01:23,281
Of these, compression of nerve ganglia in the cervix and lower uterine segment by the contracting myometrium is an especially attractive hypothesis.

426
01:01:23,281 --> 01:01:30,418
Paracervical infiltration with local anesthetic usually produces appreciable pain relief with contractions .

427
01:01:30,418 --> 01:01:37,243
Uterine contractions are involuntary and, for the most part, independent of extrauterine control.

428
01:01:37,243 --> 01:01:43,331
Neural blockade from epidural analgesia does not diminish their frequency or intensity.

429
01:01:43,331 --> 01:01:52,706
In other examples, myometrial contractions in paraplegic women and in women after bilateral lumbar sympathectomy are normal but painless.

430
01:01:52,706 --> 01:01:59,543
Mechanical stretching of the cervix enhances uterine activity in several species, including humans.

431
01:01:59,543 --> 01:02:02,856
This phenomenon is the Ferguson reflex .

432
01:02:02,856 --> 01:02:09,431
Its exact mechanism is unclear, and release of oxytocin has been suggested but not proven.

433
01:02:09,431 --> 01:02:18,568
Manipulation of the cervix and “stripping” the fetal membranes are associated with a rise in blood levels of prostaglandin F2α metabolites.

434
01:02:18,568 --> 01:02:28,493
The interval between contractions narrows gradually from approximately 10 minutes at the onset of first-stage labor to as little as 1 minute or less in the second stage.

435
01:02:28,493 --> 01:02:34,693
Periods of relaxation between contractions, however, are essential for fetal welfare.

436
01:02:34,693 --> 01:02:41,568
Unremitting contractions compromise uteroplacental blood flow sufficiently to cause fetal hypoxemia.

437
01:02:41,568 --> 01:02:49,081
In activephase labor, the duration of each contraction ranges from 30 to 90 seconds and averages 1 minute.

438
01:02:49,081 --> 01:02:53,356
Contraction intensity varies appreciably during normal labor.

439
01:02:53,356 --> 01:03:05,081
Specifically, amnionic fluid pressures generated by contractions during spontaneous labor average 40 mm Hg, but vary from 20 to 60 mm Hg .

440
01:03:05,081 --> 01:03:18,931
Distinct Lower and Upper Uterine Segments During active labor, the anatomical uterine divisions that were initiated in phase 2 of parturition become increasingly evident (Figs. 21-11 and 21-12).

441
01:03:18,931 --> 01:03:26,118
By abdominal palpation, even before membrane rupture, the two segments can sometimes be differentiated.

442
01:03:26,118 --> 01:03:33,656
The upper segment is firm during contractions, whereas the lower segment is softer, distended, and more passive.

443
01:03:33,656 --> 01:03:35,818
This mechanism is

444
01:03:35,980 --> 01:03:48,505
imperative because if the entire myometrium, including the lower uterine segment and cervix, were to contract simultaneously and with equal intensity, the net expulsive force would markedly decline.

445
01:03:48,455 --> 01:03:53,705
Thus, the upper segment contracts, retracts, and expels the fetus.

446
01:03:53,705 --> 01:04:04,305
In response to these contractions, the softened lower uterine segment and cervix dilate and thereby form a greatly expanded, thinned-out tube through which the fetus can pass.

447
01:04:04,305 --> 01:04:06,930
Figure 21-11.

448
01:04:06,930 --> 01:04:12,092
Sequence of development of the segments and rings in the uterus at term and in labor.

449
01:04:12,092 --> 01:04:18,542
Note comparison between the nonpregnant uterus, the uterus at term, and the uterus during labor.

450
01:04:18,542 --> 01:04:28,317
The passive lower uterine segment is derived from the isthmus, and the physiological retraction ring develops at the junction of the upper and lower uterine segments.

451
01:04:28,317 --> 01:04:33,105
The pathological retraction ring develops from the physiological ring.

452
01:04:33,105 --> 01:04:46,455
Anat. I.O. = anatomical internal os; E.O. = external os; Hist. I.O. = histological internal os; Ph.R.R. = physiological retraction ring.

453
01:04:46,455 --> 01:04:49,117
Figure 21-12.

454
01:04:49,117 --> 01:04:52,392
The uterus at the time of vaginal delivery.

455
01:04:52,392 --> 01:04:58,555
The active upper segment retracts around the presenting part as the fetus descends through the birth canal.

456
01:04:58,555 --> 01:05:03,667
In the passive lower segment, there is considerably less myometrial tone.

457
01:05:03,667 --> 01:05:09,705
The myometrium of the upper segment does not relax to its original length after contractions.

458
01:05:09,705 --> 01:05:14,042
Instead, it becomes relatively fixed at a shorter length.

459
01:05:14,042 --> 01:05:22,117
The upper active uterine segment contracts down on its diminishing contents, but myometrial tension remains constant.

460
01:05:22,117 --> 01:05:28,505
The net effect is to take up slack, thus maintaining the advantage gained in expulsion of the fetus.

461
01:05:28,505 --> 01:05:34,617
Concurrently, the uterine musculature is kept in firm contact with the uterine contents.

462
01:05:34,617 --> 01:05:41,305
As the consequence of retraction, each successive contraction commences where its predecessor left off.

463
01:05:41,305 --> 01:05:47,755
Thus, the upper part of the uterine cavity becomes slightly smaller with each successive contraction.

464
01:05:47,755 --> 01:05:59,030
Because of the successive shortening of the muscular fibers, the upper active segment becomes progressively thickened throughout first- and second-stage labor (see Fig. 21-11).

465
01:05:59,030 --> 01:06:06,217
This process continues and results in a tremendously thickened upper uterine segment immediately after delivery.

466
01:06:06,217 --> 01:06:15,117
Clinically, it is important to understand that the phenomenon of upper segment retraction is contingent on a decrease in the volume of its contents.

467
01:06:15,117 --> 01:06:26,542
For this to happen, particularly early in labor when the entire uterus is virtually a closed sac with only minimal cervical dilation, the musculature of the lower segment must stretch.

468
01:06:26,542 --> 01:06:31,992
This permits a greater portion of the uterine contents to occupy the lower segment.

469
01:06:31,992 --> 01:06:38,380
The upper segment retracts only to the extent that the lower segment distends and the cervix dilates.

470
01:06:38,380 --> 01:06:44,292
Relaxation of the lower uterine segment mirrors the same gradual progression of retraction.

471
01:06:44,292 --> 01:06:53,242
In the lower segment, successive lengthening of the fibers with labor is accompanied by thinning, normally to only a few millimeters in the thinnest part.

472
01:06:53,242 --> 01:07:04,042
With lower segment thinning and concomitant upper segment thickening, a boundary between the two is marked by a ridge on the inner uterine surface—the physiological retraction ring.

473
01:07:04,042 --> 01:07:13,005
When the thinning of the lower uterine segment is extreme, as in obstructed labor, the ring is prominent and forms a pathological retraction ring.

474
01:07:13,005 --> 01:07:21,130
This abnormal condition is also known as the Bandl ring, which is discussed further in Chapter 23 (p. 442).

475
01:07:21,130 --> 01:07:31,717
Changes in Uterine Shape Each contraction gradually elongates the ovoid uterine shape an estimated 5 to 10 cm and thereby narrows the horizontal diameter.

476
01:07:31,717 --> 01:07:36,005
This fetal axis pressure straightens the fetal vertebral column.

477
01:07:36,005 --> 01:07:43,567
It also presses the upper pole of the fetus firmly against the fundus, whereas the lower pole is thrust farther downward.

478
01:07:43,567 --> 01:07:48,592
With uterine lengthening, the longitudinal muscle fibers are drawn taut.

479
01:07:48,592 --> 01:07:58,080
As a result, the lower segment and cervix are the only parts of the uterus that are flexible, and these are pulled upward and around the lower pole of the fetus.

480
01:07:58,080 --> 01:08:05,130
Ancillary Forces After the cervix is dilated fully, the most important force in fetal expulsion is

481
01:08:05,284 --> 01:08:08,634
produced by maternal intraabdominal pressure.

482
01:08:08,584 --> 01:08:16,484
Contraction of the abdominal muscles simultaneously with forced respiratory efforts with the glottis closed is referred to as pushing.

483
01:08:16,484 --> 01:08:22,084
The force is similar to that with defecation, but the intensity usually is much greater.

484
01:08:22,084 --> 01:08:30,821
The importance of intraabdominal pressure is shown by the prolonged descent during labor in paraplegic women and in those with a dense epidural block.

485
01:08:30,821 --> 01:08:39,634
Cervical Changes As the result of contraction forces, two fundamental changes—effacement and dilation—occur in the ripened cervix.

486
01:08:39,634 --> 01:08:48,746
For an average-sized fetal head to pass through the cervix, it must completely or fully dilate to a diameter of approximately 10 cm.

487
01:08:48,746 --> 01:08:52,546
The fetus may not descend during cervical effacement.

488
01:08:52,546 --> 01:08:58,659
However, as the cervix dilates, the presenting fetal part typically does descend somewhat.

489
01:08:58,659 --> 01:09:03,159
Cervical effacement is “obliteration” or “taking up” of the cervix.

490
01:09:03,159 --> 01:09:13,071
It is manifest clinically by shortening of the cervical canal from a length of approximately 3 cm to a mere circular orifice with almost paper-thin edges.

491
01:09:13,071 --> 01:09:21,259
The muscular fibers at the level of the internal cervical os are pulled upward, or “taken up,” into the lower uterine segment.

492
01:09:21,259 --> 01:09:25,946
The condition of the external os remains temporarily unchanged .

493
01:09:25,946 --> 01:09:36,796
Because of growing myometrial activity during uterine preparedness for labor, appreciable effacement of a softened cervix sometimes is accomplished before active labor begins.

494
01:09:36,796 --> 01:09:42,121
Effacement causes expulsion of the mucous plug as the cervical canal is shortened.

495
01:09:42,121 --> 01:09:45,021
Figure 21-13.

496
01:09:45,021 --> 01:09:48,321
Schematic showing effacement and dilation.

497
01:09:48,321 --> 01:09:58,334
A. Before labor, the primigravid cervix is long and undilated in contrast to that of the multipara, which has dilation of the internal and external os.

498
01:09:58,334 --> 01:10:07,321
B. As effacement begins, the multiparous cervix shows more dilation and funneling of the internal os compared with the primigravid cervix.

499
01:10:07,321 --> 01:10:13,409
C. As complete effacement is achieved in the primigravid cervix, dilation is minimal.

500
01:10:13,409 --> 01:10:16,334
The reverse is true in the multipara.

501
01:10:16,334 --> 01:10:25,946
Because the lower segment and cervix have less resistance during a contraction, a centrifugal pull is exerted on the cervix and creates cervical dilation .

502
01:10:25,946 --> 01:10:35,584
As uterine contractions cause pressure on the membranes, the hydrostatic action of the amnionic sac in turn dilates the cervical canal like a wedge.

503
01:10:35,584 --> 01:10:42,159
The process of cervical effacement and dilation causes formation of the forebag of amnionic fluid.

504
01:10:42,159 --> 01:10:48,121
This is the leading portion of fluid and amnionic sac located in front of the presenting part.

505
01:10:48,121 --> 01:10:56,846
In the absence of intact membranes, the pressure of the presenting fetal part against the cervix and lower uterine segment is a similar wedge.

506
01:10:56,846 --> 01:11:06,921
Early rupture of the membranes does not retard cervical dilation so long as the presenting fetal part is positioned to exert pressure against the cervix and lower segment.

507
01:11:06,921 --> 01:11:09,746
Figure 21-14.

508
01:11:09,746 --> 01:11:15,121
Hydrostatic action of membranes in effecting cervical effacement and dilation.

509
01:11:15,121 --> 01:11:22,571
With labor progression, note the changing relations of the internal and external os in (A), (B), and (C).

510
01:11:22,571 --> 01:11:32,009
Although not shown in this diagram, with membrane rupture, the presenting part, applied to the cervix and the forming lower uterine segment, acts similarly.

511
01:11:32,009 --> 01:11:39,696
Referring back to Figure 21-4, recall that cervical dilation is divided into latent and active phases.

512
01:11:39,696 --> 01:11:45,284
The duration of the latent phase is more variable and sensitive to extraneous factors.

513
01:11:45,284 --> 01:11:51,971
For example, sedation may prolong the latent phase, and myometrial stimulation shortens it.

514
01:11:51,971 --> 01:12:01,696
The latent phase duration has little bearing on the subsequent course of labor, whereas the characteristics of the active phase are usually predictive of labor outcome.

515
01:12:01,696 --> 01:12:07,621
Normal and abnormal labor curves are fully described in Chapters 22 and 23.

516
01:12:07,621 --> 01:12:11,059
# Second Stage: Fetal Descent.

517
01:12:11,059 --> 01:12:16,571
In many nulliparas, engagement of the head is accomplished before labor begins.

518
01:12:16,571 --> 01:12:21,096
That said, the head may not descend further until late in labor.

519
01:12:21,096 --> 01:12:32,296
In the descent pattern of normal labor, a typical hyperbolic curve is formed when the station of the fetal head is plotted as a function of labor duration (see Fig. 21-4).

520
01:12:32,296 --> 01:12:36,071
Station describes descent of the fetal biparietal

521
01:12:36,220 --> 01:12:41,145
diameter in relation to a line drawn between maternal ischial spines .

522
01:12:41,095 --> 01:12:46,345
Active descent usually takes place after dilation has progressed for some time.

523
01:12:46,345 --> 01:12:54,395
During second-stage labor, the speed of descent is maximal and is maintained until the presenting part reaches the perineal floor .

524
01:12:54,395 --> 01:12:59,432
In nulliparas, the presenting part typically descends slowly and steadily.

525
01:12:59,432 --> 01:13:03,282
In multiparas, however, descent may be rapid.

526
01:13:03,282 --> 01:13:06,120
# Pelvic Floor Changes.

527
01:13:06,120 --> 01:13:10,832
The birth canal is supported and functionally closed by the pelvic floor .

528
01:13:10,832 --> 01:13:19,782
The most important component of the floor is the levator ani muscle and the fibromuscular connective tissue that covers its upper and lower surfaces.

529
01:13:19,782 --> 01:13:26,545
The biomechanical properties of these structures and of the vaginal wall change markedly during parturition.

530
01:13:26,545 --> 01:13:36,282
These result from altered ECM structure or composition (Alperin, 2015; Lowder, 2007; Rahn, 2008).

531
01:13:36,282 --> 01:13:43,770
In the first stage of labor, the membranes, when intact, and the fetal presenting part serve to dilate the upper vagina.

532
01:13:43,770 --> 01:13:48,682
The most marked change consists of stretching levator ani muscle fibers.

533
01:13:48,682 --> 01:14:02,057
This is accompanied by thinning of the central portion of the perineum, which becomes transformed from a wedge-shaped, 5-cm-thick tissue mass to a thin, almost transparent membranous structure less than 1 cm thick.

534
01:14:02,057 --> 01:14:14,432
When the perineum is distended maximally, the anus becomes markedly dilated and presents an opening that varies from 2 to 3 cm in diameter and through which the anterior wall of the rectum bulges.

535
01:14:14,432 --> 01:14:19,045
# Third Stage: Delivery of Placenta and Membranes.

536
01:14:19,045 --> 01:14:26,782
This stage begins immediately after fetal delivery and involves separation and expulsion of the placenta and membranes.

537
01:14:26,782 --> 01:14:38,295
Normally, by the time the newborn is delivered, the uterine cavity is nearly obliterated and is an almost solid mass of muscle, several centimeters thick, above the thinner lower segment.

538
01:14:38,295 --> 01:14:42,907
The uterine fundus now lies just below the level of the umbilicus.

539
01:14:42,907 --> 01:14:50,845
This sudden diminution in uterine size is inevitably accompanied by a decrease in the area of the placental implantation site .

540
01:14:50,845 --> 01:14:59,270
For the placenta to accommodate itself to this reduced area, it thickens, but because of limited placental elasticity, it buckles.

541
01:14:59,270 --> 01:15:05,082
The resulting tension pulls the weakest layer—decidua spongiosa—from that site.

542
01:15:05,082 --> 01:15:14,820
Thus, placental separation follows the disproportion created between the relatively unchanged placental size and the reduced implantation site size.

543
01:15:14,820 --> 01:15:17,607
Figure 21-15.

544
01:15:17,607 --> 01:15:22,107
Diminution in size of the placental site after birth of the newborn.

545
01:15:22,107 --> 01:15:25,132
A. Spatial relations before birth.

546
01:15:25,132 --> 01:15:28,757
B. Placental spatial relations after birth.

547
01:15:28,757 --> 01:15:34,307
Cleavage of the placenta is aided greatly by the loose structure of the spongy decidua.

548
01:15:34,307 --> 01:15:43,545
As detachment proceeds, a hematoma forms between the separating placenta and the adjacent decidua, which remains attached to the myometrium.

549
01:15:43,545 --> 01:15:51,195
The hematoma is usually the result rather than the cause of the separation, because in some cases bleeding is negligible.

550
01:15:51,195 --> 01:16:01,820
The great decline in uterine cavity surface area simultaneously throws the fetal membranes—the amniochorion and the parietal decidua—into innumerable folds .

551
01:16:01,820 --> 01:16:07,270
Membranes usually remain in situ until placental separation is nearly completed.

552
01:16:07,270 --> 01:16:18,457
These are then peeled off the uterine wall, partly by further contraction of the myometrium and partly by traction that is exerted by the separated placenta as it descends during expulsion.

553
01:16:18,457 --> 01:16:21,295
Figure 21-16.

554
01:16:21,295 --> 01:16:27,345
Postpartum, membranes are thrown up into folds as the uterine cavity decreases in size.

555
01:16:27,345 --> 01:16:31,457
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Kelley S. Carrick.

556
01:16:31,457 --> 01:16:37,070
After the placenta has detached, it can be expelled by increased abdominal pressure.

557
01:16:37,070 --> 01:16:46,570
Completion of the third stage is also accomplished by alternately compressing and elevating the fundus, while exerting minimal traction on the umbilical cord.

558
01:16:46,570 --> 01:16:53,907
The retroplacental hematoma either follows the placenta or is found within the inverted sac formed by the membranes.

559
01:16:53,907 --> 01:17:05,782
In this process, known as the Schultze mechanism of placental expulsion, blood from the placental site pours into the membrane sac and does not escape externally until after extrusion of the placenta.

560
01:17:05,782 --> 01:17:08,882
In the other form of placental extrusion,

561
01:17:09,052 --> 01:17:18,452
known as the Duncan mechanism, the placenta separates first at the periphery and blood collects between the membranes and the uterine wall and escapes from the vagina.

562
01:17:18,402 --> 01:17:24,839
In this circumstance, the placenta descends sideways, and its maternal surface appears first.

563
01:17:24,839 --> 01:17:29,439
UTEROTONINS IN PARTURITION PHASE 3 # Oxytocin.

564
01:17:29,439 --> 01:17:36,639
This nanopeptide is synthesized in the magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular neurons.

565
01:17:36,639 --> 01:17:48,677
The prohormone is transported with its carrier protein, neurophysin, along the axons to the neural lobe of the posterior pituitary gland in membrane-bound vesicles for storage and later release.

566
01:17:48,677 --> 01:17:57,089
The prohormone is converted enzymatically to oxytocin during transport (Gainer, 1988; Leake, 1990).

567
01:17:57,089 --> 01:18:04,552
Oxytocin—literally, quick birth—was the first uterotonin to be implicated in parturition initiation.

568
01:18:04,552 --> 01:18:29,664
Support for this role includes: (1) oxytocin receptor numbers strikingly rising in myometrial and decidual tissues near the end of gestation; (2) oxytocin acting on decidual tissue to promote prostaglandin release; and (3) oxytocin synthesis directly in decidual and extraembryonic fetal tissues and in the placenta (Chibbar, 1993; Zingg, 1995).

569
01:18:29,664 --> 01:18:38,952
Moreover, abundant data support oxytocin’s important role during second-stage labor and in the puerperium, which is phase 4 of parturition.

570
01:18:38,952 --> 01:18:52,427
Specifically, maternal serum oxytocin levels are elevated: (1) during second-stage labor, which is the end of phase 3 of parturition; (2) in the early puerperium; and (3) during breastfeeding .

571
01:18:52,427 --> 01:19:05,089
Immediately after delivery of the fetus and placenta, which completes parturition phase 3, firm and persistent uterine contractions induced by oxytocin are essential to prevent postpartum hemorrhage.

572
01:19:05,089 --> 01:19:07,714
# Prostaglandins.

573
01:19:07,714 --> 01:19:12,464
Prostaglandins play a critical role in phase 3 of parturition .

574
01:19:12,464 --> 01:19:22,052
First, levels of prostaglandins—or their metabolites—in amnionic fluid, maternal plasma, and maternal urine are increased during labor.

575
01:19:22,052 --> 01:19:29,102
Second, receptors for PGE2 and PGF2α are expressed in the uterus and cervix.

576
01:19:29,102 --> 01:19:34,152
Thus, if these tissues are exposed to prostaglandins, they will respond.

577
01:19:34,152 --> 01:19:43,839
Third, treatment of pregnant women with prostaglandins, by any of several administration routes, causes abortion or labor at all gestational ages.

578
01:19:43,839 --> 01:19:54,564
Moreover, administration of prostaglandin H synthase type 2 inhibitors to pregnant women will delay spontaneous labor onset and sometimes arrest preterm labor .

579
01:19:54,564 --> 01:20:02,852
During labor, prostaglandin production within the myometrium and decidua is an efficient mechanism of activating contractions.

580
01:20:02,852 --> 01:20:10,764
For example, prostaglandin synthesis is high and unchanging in the decidua during phase 2 and 3 of parturition.

581
01:20:10,764 --> 01:20:22,427
Moreover, the receptor level for PGF2α is augmented in the decidua at term, and this increase most likely is the regulatory step in prostaglandin action in the uterus.

582
01:20:22,427 --> 01:20:26,877
The fetal membranes and placenta also produce prostaglandins.

583
01:20:26,877 --> 01:20:35,189
Primarily PGE2, but also PGF2α, are detected in amnionic fluid at all gestational ages.

584
01:20:35,189 --> 01:20:41,064
As the fetus grows, prostaglandin levels in the amnionic fluid rise gradually.

585
01:20:41,064 --> 01:20:48,664
Their greatest elevation in concentration within amnionic fluid, however, is demonstrable after labor begins.

586
01:20:48,664 --> 01:20:54,502
These higher levels likely result as the cervix dilates and exposes decidual tissue .

587
01:20:54,502 --> 01:21:04,927
These higher levels in the forebag, compared with those in the upper compartment, are believed to follow an inflammatory response that signals the events leading to active labor.

588
01:21:04,927 --> 01:21:13,477
Together, the rise in cytokine and prostaglandin concentrations further degrade the ECM, thus weakening fetal membranes.

589
01:21:13,477 --> 01:21:16,402
Figure 21-17.

590
01:21:16,402 --> 01:21:23,339
Sagittal view of the exposed forebag and attached decidual fragments after cervical dilation during labor.

591
01:21:23,339 --> 01:21:28,289
Redrawn from MacDonald PC, Casey ML: Preterm birth.

592
01:21:28,289 --> 01:21:32,364
Sci Am 3:42, 1996.

593
01:21:32,364 --> 01:21:34,789
# Endothelin 1.

594
01:21:34,789 --> 01:21:42,089
The endothelins are a family of 21-amino-acid peptides that powerfully induce myometrial contraction .

595
01:21:42,089 --> 01:21:50,989
The endothelin A receptor is preferentially expressed in smooth muscle, and when activated, it effects a rise in intracellular calcium.

596
01:21:50,989 --> 01:21:59,264
Endothelin 1 is produced in myometrium of term gestations and is able to induce synthesis of other contractile factors such as

597
01:21:59,428 --> 01:22:06,915
prostaglandins and inflammatory mediators (Momohara, 2004; Sutcliffe, 2009).

598
01:22:06,865 --> 01:22:13,178
The requirement of endothelin 1 in normal parturition physiology remains to be established.

599
01:22:13,178 --> 01:22:15,940
# Angiotensin II.

600
01:22:15,940 --> 01:22:23,240
Modulation of uteroplacental blood flow is regulated by angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor.

601
01:22:23,240 --> 01:22:32,878
Although, in pregnancy, circulating levels of angiotensin II are increased, vascular resistance is reduced and vasodilation is enhanced.

602
01:22:32,878 --> 01:22:39,065
Two angiotensin II receptors are expressed in the uterus—AT1R and AT2R.

603
01:22:39,065 --> 01:22:47,265
In nonpregnant women, the AT1R receptor predominates, but the AT2R receptor is preferentially expressed in gravidas .

604
01:22:47,265 --> 01:22:59,453
During normotensive pregnancy, AT2R-mediated effects on vascular smooth muscle lead to vasodilation, which contributes to the pregnancy-associated rise in uterine arterial blood flow.

605
01:22:59,453 --> 01:23:08,590
Decreased AT2R expression is associated with preeclamptic pregnancies (Mishra, 2018; Rosenfeld, 2012) .

606
01:23:08,590 --> 01:23:17,128
PHASE 4: THE PUERPERIUM Immediately and for about an hour after delivery, the myometrium remains persistently contracted.

607
01:23:17,128 --> 01:23:23,740
This directly compresses large uterine vessels and allows thrombosis of their lumens to prevent hemorrhage.

608
01:23:23,740 --> 01:23:29,515
This is typically augmented by endogenous and pharmacological uterotonic agents .

609
01:23:29,515 --> 01:23:37,090
Uterine involution and cervical repair are prompt remodeling processes that restore these organs to the nonpregnant state.

610
01:23:37,090 --> 01:23:46,515
These protect the reproductive tract from invasion by commensal microorganisms and restore endometrial responsiveness to normal hormonal cyclicity.

611
01:23:46,515 --> 01:23:52,503
During the early puerperium, lactogenesis and milk let-down begin in mammary glands .

612
01:23:52,503 --> 01:23:57,665
Reinstitution of ovulation signals preparation for the next pregnancy.

613
01:23:57,665 --> 01:24:02,315
Ovulation generally occurs within 4 to 6 weeks after birth.

614
01:24:02,315 --> 01:24:10,553
However, it is dependent on the duration of breastfeeding and lactation-induced, prolactin-mediated anovulation and amenorrhea.

